    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The CA2 region of the hippocampus plays a vital role in the storage and recall of social memory in mice, but the neural mechanism for this memory storage remains unknown. While the CA2 region remained largely unstudied for years, recent findings give evidence that this region is necessary for social memories to form, and CA2 pyramidal cells contain the machinery necessary to function in social memory. Place cells are a specific type of pyramidal cell found in the CA2 of the hippocampus, and previous research links them to involvement in social memory. This study’s goal was to investigate if place cells are responsible for the CA2’s role in social memory and to identify specific mechanisms that place cells may use to store this information. To analyze the properties and changes that occur in place cells in the presence of a social stimulus, tetrodes, small wire-devices used to record from live cells, were implanted into the mice hippocampi to record from CA2 place cells while the mice underwent controlled-environment, social-stimulus behavioral experiments. This research revealed that in social situations CA2 place cells coherently remap as subpopulations and reduce the mean firing field size. These data reveal that place cells in the CA2 of the hippocampus do, in fact, respond to social stimuli in a way that is typical for hippocampal cells encoding memory in mice and gives insight into how these cells may function in storing this social information. In addition to expanding our knowledge of the CA2 region of the hippocampus, a better understanding of social memory can lead to a better understanding of diseases that affect social memory or cause damage to the hippocampus, such as Autism.",
